WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:03.210
WESLEY CHUN: Hey, welcome back
to our series on using G Suite

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:04.980
developer tools for
student developers.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.340
In this segment, we'll look at
using G Suite REST APIs along

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.360
with code samples.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.220
I'm your host, Wesley Chun.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:12.690
If you missed the
overview video,

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.600
check it out first because
there we show you how to get

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.210
started, getting credentials
and creating projects

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.710
using G Suite APIs.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:23.070
Now we're going to dive in,
starting with Google Drive.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.130
Here's code that lists the
first 100 files and folders

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.270
in your Google Drive.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.370
Believe it or not,
the real application

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.710
is really just the
last three lines.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.810
Everything else is just
the security boilerplate

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.870
we covered earlier plus a
requested scopes and API

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.520
endpoint creation.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.380
Once you have an endpoint
to the Drive API,

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:44.400
it's as simple as creating a
file listing query, executing

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.550
that query, and
displaying the results.

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:48.300
If you want to build
this app line by line

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.620
check out the Code Map.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.590
Heads up, Google's upgrading
to newer client libraries

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.800
to bring the GCP and G Suite
worlds closer together.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.830
While the current libraries will
stay operational for a while,

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.850
you need to see how
the code changes.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.260
So here's the exact same example
but with the newer libraries.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.540
As you can see, aside from
a couple of import changes,

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.580
managing the OAuth
tokens yourself,

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.970
everything else stays
mostly the same.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:13.980
The G Suite docs already
reflect this change

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.230
but bear in mind that most
of the code in the wild

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.990
still uses the original
libraries, including

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.300
the deep dive video linked here.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:22.770
While this sample
is in Python, you

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.145
can guess that
the client library

00:01:24.145 --> 00:01:26.370
upgrades like this apply
regardless of what language

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:27.250
you use.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.250
OK, now that you've
seen some code let's

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:32.780
take a step back and think why
you would use the Drive API?

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.680
Well, one reason
could be, you want

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.330
to write an app that
backs up zip files

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.730
but expands them in
flight to Google Drive.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.080
Well, this is an actual
JavaScript sample app.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:44.020
Check out its open source
repo if interested.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.680
Another example, let's say you
get a job at a startup helping

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.340
people automate photo albums.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.360
Well, you'd pitch that everyone
who gets back from vacation

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.370
have to empty out their
cameras and phones

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:54.480
to an external hard drive.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.090
And what do all those
pictures look like on disk?

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.700
A big unorganized mess, right?

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.700
But if you use the Drive
API to access file metadata

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.760
like the timestamp and
geolocation, you and your team

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.750
could actually build an app that
auto generates photo albums!

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.660
Now taking it a step further,
stitch those photos together

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.840
into a video, upload it
with the YouTube API,

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.350
then use the Gmail API to
tell your friends and family.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:23.040
Get an idea of what you can
do with Google APIs now?

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.890
It's as limitless
as your imagination.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.860
Each Google API has
official documentation.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.610
And the G Suite ones live at
developers.google.com/ whatever

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.070
the API name is, such as Drive.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:37.800
The docs are structured
via guides tab that

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.230
feature quick starts
in multiple languages

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:43.410
as well as guides for
specific API features.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:46.020
Now beyond the quick starts
are more fully fleshed

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.160
out sample apps.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.250
These live under the
samples tab for each API

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:53.940
and links to any open source
repos or developer videos.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.830
Now if you need
help, the Support tab

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:57.930
links to stack
overflow, or issue

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.240
tracker for bugs and
feature requests,

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.690
or perhaps communities of
like minded developers.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:05.940
Next, let's move
to the Sheets API.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:08.640
After all, aren't spreadsheets
the most programmable

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.560
of all the productivity apps?

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.540
You may have data sitting
in a database or a CSV file,

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:15.810
but those form
factors aren't exactly

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.610
visually appealing, right?

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.190
Do you really want to tell
your boss to select star

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:21.640
from your database?

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.570
Well, spreadsheets are a common
and simple user interface

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:26.280
that feature tabular data.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.740
And JavaScript developers
can try the API code

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.750
lab that lets you build
a customized reporting

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.440
tool for a fictitious
toy manufacturer.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:34.940
Now in this code
sample, we're going

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:37.350
to reuse that toy order
database and migrate it

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:38.700
to a spreadsheet.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.230
Again, the OAuth code will
be the same as before,

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:44.880
but switched with the Sheet's
rewrite scope and its API

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.140
endpoint.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.540
Now as you can see, the first
half of the code snippet

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.510
queries for the toy order
data while the second half

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.180
creates a new sheet and
pushes the data there.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.760
Check out the video for
more on this sample.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.550
Don't forget to
enable the API first!

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.990
Moving on, why the Slides API?

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:05.700
Well the same reason as Sheets
but taking it a step further.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.340
Yes, Sheets is definitely
more visual than a database

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.910
but isn't a slide deck the
ultimate in visualization?

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:15.030
Or maybe you're organizing
your school's hackathon

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.550
and want to create only
one master slide deck when

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.529
seeking corporate sponsorships.

00:04:19.529 --> 00:04:21.750
Or you're a sales person
who's giving the same pitch

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:23.160
to multiple clients.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.680
Well, why not create one
master template deck,

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.630
then auto generate
customized decks

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:30.120
for each sponsor or customer?

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.670
Now here's that code snippet,
replacing the placeholder

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:35.400
variables with actual data,
whether it's the customer

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.360
name or corporate logo.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.170
Now this one's also got a
video for further study.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:41.730
In real life you
can probably guess

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:42.870
this isn't the whole thing.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:45.580
And you will need to
use more than one API.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:46.630
Well, think about it.

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:48.390
Don't you have to
call the Drive API

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:51.135
to copy the master template,
then get the customer

00:04:51.135 --> 00:04:54.060
or sponsor information
from the Sheets API,

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.370
then generate the slide
deck with the Slides API?

00:04:57.370 --> 00:04:59.520
Well, this pseudocode
answers the obvious question

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.520
of how to set up using more
than one API at a time.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.710
Now here's another
example using two APIs.

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:08.800
Here, we take data and a
chart from a spreadsheet

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.600
and add them to a slide deck.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:13.810
The video also shows how to read
the data from the Sheets API

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:15.310
first.

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:17.200
Now the Docs API
is a perfect tool

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:19.300
for creating a mail merge tool.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.250
Similar to the
customer Slide decks,

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:23.740
let's say you got a form letter
with variable placeholders

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.040
like this.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:27.850
Then you have a sheet with
the data for each recipient.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:30.280
Well, here's the heart
of the mail merge code.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:33.280
Look how similar it is
to the Slides example.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.380
Once you copy the
master template doc

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:37.060
and read the data
from the sheet,

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:40.570
it's not much effort to fill
in those variable placeholders.

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:42.490
Now this video doesn't
go over the code

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:45.532
but you can get a link to
the app's open source repo.

00:05:45.532 --> 00:05:46.990
Once it's done
executing you should

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:49.810
have an individual Google Doc
with all the substitutions

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:50.860
made.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:54.520
All you need to do is call the
Drive API to export it as PDF,

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:57.100
then build an email message
with this attachment,

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.810
and then send it
with the Gmail API.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:03.310
All right, so that's our quick
tour of G Suite REST APIs.

